{{Hiero/2cartouche|align=right|era=mk|name=Senusret III|praenomen=<hiero>F12-s-D21:t-z:#:n</hiero>|nomen=<hiero>ra-N28-D28*D28:D28</hiero>}} '''Senusret III''' was a [[pharaoh]] of [[Ancient Egypt|Egypt]]. He ruled from [[1878 BC]] to [[1839 BC]], and was the fifth monarch of the [[Twelfth dynasty of Egypt|Twelfth Dynasty]] of the [[Middle Kingdom]]. He was a Great Pharaoh of the twelfth Dynasty and is supposed to be the most powerful Egyptian ruler of this time. This for, his activity was one of the sourses of the legend about [[Sesostris]].
Senusret III continued his Kingdom's expansion deep into Nubia (from 1866 to 1863 BC) where he erected massive River Forts including Bukhen and Toshka at Uronarti. His pyramid was constructed at [[Dahshur]], he also built the Labyrinth of Phayum and the new capital city, It-Taui. A papyrus in the [[Berlin]] Museum shows Year 20 of his reign is equivalent to Year 1 of his son [[Amenemhat III]]. This means that he initiated a coregency with his son in this year. A stone block recovered ''in situ'' from the ruins of his Funerary Temple at Abydos suggests that he was still alive in his thirty ninth Regnal Year. This implies that he served as the senior coregent in his son's reign for almost two decades prior to his death.
